To run Movian from source with the WebAssembly build:


   source emsdk/emsdk_env.sh
   ./configure.wasm
   make BUILD=wasm stage

To build a debug-focused wasm stage with stronger runtime diagnostics:

   make BUILD=wasm dbgstage

`dbgstage` enables Emscripten runtime checks (assertions, safe heap,
stack-overflow checks, and emulated function pointer casts) to make
`RuntimeError: function signature mismatch` crashes easier to pinpoint.
It also keeps function names in stack traces and writes `build.wasm/movian.symbols`
for mapping `funcNNNN` entries to C symbols.

Then serve the staged directory with thread-required isolation headers:

   python3 support/wasm/serve_stage.py --dir build.wasm/stage --port 8000

If you still see `thread pool is exhausted`, rebuild with a larger pool:

   make BUILD=wasm dbgstage WASM_PTHREAD_POOL_SIZE=24

Open http://localhost:8000/index.html in a browser.

In browser DevTools console, verify thread prerequisites:

   window.crossOriginIsolated
   typeof SharedArrayBuffer !== 'undefined'

`python3 -m http.server` is not enough for pthread-enabled WASM builds because
it does not add COOP/COEP response headers, so `SharedArrayBuffer` stays
disabled and thread creation fails.

If your page loads cross-origin assets, those assets must also be embeddable
under `Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp` (CORS or CORP headers).

Do not open `index.html` directly via `file://`.
The WebAssembly runtime fetches `movian.wasm` and other assets, which modern
browsers block from local file URLs.

The old `background.js` and `manifest.json` files are Chrome App / NaCl leftovers
and are not part of the normal WASM startup path anymore.

For more key bindings see
 https://movian.tv/projects/showtime/wiki/Key_mappings
